## 3\. Rules

### 3.1 Board

* **Hex grid, radius R = 8** (217 cells).
* **Boundary policy: WRAP (toroidal).** A ship leaving one edge reappears on the opposite edge.
* Renderer makes the wrap visually obvious: ships that straddle the edge are drawn on both sides simultaneously.

### 3.2 Fleet

* Standard 5/4/3/3/2 fleet (17 ship cells, \~7.8% board density).
* Each ship is a contiguous line of hexes on placement.

### 3.3 Ship State

Each ship carries a **(shape, position, heading, cooldown)** tuple:

* **shape**: the cells currently alive (degrades with damage)
* **position**: where its body is right now
* **heading**: one of 6 hex directions (hidden from opponent)
* **cooldown**: turns between moves (starts at 2 — "moves every 2 turns")

### 3.4 Placement (Defender)

* Defender chooses **heading + cooldown** for each ship.
* Position is auto-assigned or random — under wrap, all cells are strategically equivalent, so position alone carries no information.
* Defender's choices: where on the **course-design** axis (slow & predictable vs fast & elusive) to place each ship.

### 3.5 Movement

* Each turn, every ship's `since_move` counter increments.
* When `since_move ≥ cooldown`, the ship moves 1 hex in its heading and resets the counter.
* If a step would leave the board, the move **wraps** to the opposite edge.
* Headings do **not** change during play (defender doesn't pilot).

### 3.6 Probing & Combat

* **Salvo**: each turn the attacker fires **2 shots** (single-shot cadence is too slow vs moving targets).
* Each shot reveals **hit** or **miss** on that cell, for that turn.
* On hit: the affected cell is removed from the ship's shape, and the ship's **cooldown increases by 1** (each hit permanently slows the ship).
* When a ship's last cell is destroyed, it is **sunk** and the defender is notified.

### 3.7 Damage Mechanics (the critical innovation)
| Mechanism | Effect |
|----|----|
| Cell removed | Smaller target — *harder to hit again* |
| Cooldown +1 | Moves less often — *easier to track* |



This compound damage creates a genuine strategic tension for both players. Hits compound: a 5-ship that has taken 2 hits moves every 4 turns instead of every 2 — the attacker has bought time to triangulate.

### 3.8 Footprint Trail

* All past hits remain visible on the attacker's view as persistent X marks with **timestamps** (or whatever visual equivalent).
* The trail of timestamped hits is what the attacker uses to infer heading & speed.
* Misses are also persistent (greyed out) so the attacker doesn't waste shots.

### 3.9 Win Condition

* First player to sink the opponent's entire fleet wins.
* Optional turn cap (e.g., 200 turns) to prevent stalled games.


## 4\. Tuning Parameters (validated by simulation)
| Parameter | Value | Rationale |
|----|----|----|
| Board radius R | **8** (217 cells) | 7.8% density, first hit in 4-10 turns |
| Boundary | **WRAP** | Statistically perfect uniformity (cv ≈ 0.003); no degenerate strategies |
| Fleet | 5/4/3/3/2 | Familiar; balanced |
| Base cooldown | 2 turns/move | Fast enough to matter, slow enough to track |
| Damage→cooldown | +1 per hit | Compounds tracking advantage |
| Salvo size | 2 shots/turn | Required to keep up with movement |



## 5\. Why Wrap (and not Reverse-180° or Despawn)

Three boundary policies were considered:
| Policy | Outcome | Verdict |
|----|----|----|
| **Despawn** (ship leaves and is gone) | Ship lifetime ≈ R+1 turns → entire fleet evaporates before attacker fires 17 shots | Dead game |
| **Reverse** (180° flip on edge) | Near-uniform occupancy, but degenerate "hide in center, bounce off walls" defender strategies | Playable, but less elegant |
| **Wrap** (torus) | Perfect uniformity, all cells strategically equivalent, longer trajectories with more genuine novelty | **Selected** |



The original concern about wrap — hex-board toroidal identification requires a "twist" that's mathematically real but visually invisible — **disappears entirely in a computer game** where the renderer handles the geometry. The player never sees the twist; they just see "ships exit one edge and enter the other," exactly like Asteroids.


## 6\. UX Principles

### 6.1 First-Game Onboarding

* **Intro animation**: before the first game, show 3-4 ghost ships looping the empty board for \~20 seconds. The wrap behavior becomes self-evident through observation.
* **Tutorial first match**: a slowed-down AI opponent with one ship whose trajectory becomes visible after sinking, so the new player retroactively understands the inference loop they were doing.
* **Post-game replay**: after any ship is sunk, show that ship's complete trajectory (including all wraps) — so the player sees what their hits were actually tracking.

### 6.2 Rendering the Wrap

* When a ship's body straddles the edge, draw it **on both sides simultaneously**.
* When a ship is fully crossing, animate the transition continuously rather than teleporting.
* Footprint trails (X marks) that span a wrap should render as **two connected segments** with subtle continuity — same color, optional faint connector through off-board space.

### 6.3 Information Display
| What | How |
|----|----|
| Hit | Bright X with turn-number label |
| Miss | Greyed-out dot, optionally fading over many turns |
| Recent hits | Slightly larger / brighter than older ones |
| Inferred course | (Optional aid) faint arrow from last 2 hits showing implied heading & speed |
| Predicted next position | (Optional aid) crosshair on cell where last-hit ship is predicted to be next turn |



The optional aids should be **toggleable** — competitive players want raw signal; new players benefit from training wheels.

### 6.4 Defender Setup UI

* Defender places ships by **rotating each ship's heading arrow** (6 detent positions) and choosing cooldown (a small slider: 1-3).
* No position choice needed (under wrap, position is irrelevant) — just press *Randomize* to scatter.
* Optional: show a 10-turn preview of where each ship will be, so the defender understands their own configuration before locking in.

### 6.5 Attacker Tools

* **Trajectory ruler**: hold-and-drag a probe-line across the board, with wrap-continuation drawn live, so the attacker can sanity-check "if the ship is on this heading, would these timestamps make sense?"
* **Probe history sidebar**: scrollable list of (turn, cell, hit/miss) — sortable, filterable.
* **Active contact tracker**: when 2+ hits look correlated (same ship), the UI groups them and shows the inferred (heading, cooldown) explicitly.

### 6.6 Pacing

* Median first hit: 4-10 turns
* Median game length: 40-100 turns (target)
* Each turn should resolve in seconds, not minutes — this is a brisk inference game, not chess.


## 7\. Key Design Insights

<ol style="--ol-counter: cr-gpa2Aclbjd; counter-reset: var(--ol-counter) 0;">
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Damage-as-cooldown is the central mechanic.</b> The classic "remove cells" Battleship damage rule alone doesn't work with moving ships (smaller target, less to find). The compound "remove cell + slow ship" rule creates positive feedback that <i>rewards</i> finding a ship a second time — the trail gets easier to follow as the ship gets harder to kill.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Wrap turns position into noise and elevates course design.</b> Under wrap there is no "good place to hide." Defender strategy collapses to the fundamentals: heading and speed. This simplifies setup and deepens midgame.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>The renderer is the rulebook.</b> Most objections to wrap-style boundaries assume a static printed board. A computer game can express toroidal topology visually with zero player-side math.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>Two hits = full trajectory reconstruction.</b> Simulation showed that under either wrap or reverse, <b>two timestamped hits collapse ~5,000 prior hypotheses to a single trajectory</b> (for noise-free observations on a length-1 ship). The game's tension is therefore in <b>getting the first two hits</b>, not in solving the inference puzzle once they exist. Tune the search difficulty accordingly.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      <b>All cells equal = no degenerate strategies.</b> Under reverse, sophisticated defenders learn to exploit the geometry of bouncing trajectories near walls. Under wrap, no such exploits exist. The game is fairer and more about pure inference skill.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>


## 8\. Open Questions

* **Which R values give single-Hamiltonian-cycle trajectories?** This is number-theoretic — at some radii, straight-line wrap trajectories visit every cell before repeating; at others they form smaller subcycles. Worth picking R with intention.
* **Should headings be allowed to change mid-game?** Constant-heading is the cleanest version. Optional "captain's turn" abilities (one heading change per ship per game) could add tactical depth at the cost of inference difficulty.
* **Optimal-attacker game-length floor.** A proper Bayesian belief-tracking attacker would give a hard lower bound on game length. Heuristic attackers in simulation can't finish games at the speeds we want; needs verification with a stronger AI.
* **Asymmetric ship anatomy.** Should specific cells (engine, bow) carry specific stats so attacker targeting matters beyond "any cell of any ship"? Possible v2 expansion.
* **Multi-shot economy.** Salvo of 2 was chosen; some configurations may benefit from 3 or from "salvo size depends on remaining fleet" (classic Battleship variant precedent).




